{#
   The page's chrome, in two bands.

   Upper band: the controls, and the name of the page you are on. Lower band:
   the trail that leads to it. One band used to carry both, which squeezed the
   trail between two groups of buttons and truncated it as soon as the path grew
   - an article inside a taxonomy lost its middle.

   **The name is taken from the last crumb, not passed in.** Every page's own
   name is already the last item of its trail; asking each of the nineteen
   templates to repeat it as a `title` would be the same string written twice,
   drifting the first time one of the two is reworded. A page that wants a fuller
   name than its crumb has room for can still pass `title`, and it wins -
   "Modifier la publication" over a crumb that only says "Modifier".

   **The trail keeps every level, the page it is on included.** The first version
   of this dropped that last level, on the grounds that the band above already
   says it. That was wrong in a way only the rendered page shows: fourteen of the
   nineteen pages have exactly two crumbs, so the trail became a single word -
   the section name, which the side menu is already displaying. A one-item
   breadcrumb is not a breadcrumb, it is a label. The name showing in both bands
   is the cheaper of the two problems, and it is what every app carrying a title
   and a trail does.

   The final crumb is marked as current but stays quiet: emphasis is the upper
   band's job now.

   `strict_variables` is on, so the guard below is load-bearing: reaching for
   `.label` on an empty list throws rather than yielding nothing.
#}
{% set trail = crumbs|default([]) %}
{% set pageTitle = title|default(trail ? (trail|last).label : '') %}

<header class="lg:sticky lg:top-0 lg:z-20 shrink-0">
    {# Desktop only, all of it: below the large breakpoint the menu is a drawer
       with its own bar carrying the logo, the search and the bell, so this band
       would hold a title beside two hidden groups - 64px of border. #}
    <div class="hidden lg:flex items-center h-16 bg-surface border-b border-line">
        {# The one control that folds the side menu, at the top left of the page -
           not inside the menu it hides. A button that lives in the thing it hides
           needs a second copy somewhere to bring it back, which is what Aurora had. #}
        <div class="flex items-center gap-1 pl-3 shrink-0">
            <div {{ vue_component('core/backend/sidemenu/AppSidemenuToggle', {
                collapsedPath: path('backend_general_profile_sidemenu_collapsed'),
            }) }}></div>
            {# Back, forward and reload - browser chrome brought into the page,
               beside the control that folds the menu. #}
            <div class="border-l border-line pl-1">
                <div {{ vue_component('core/backend/sidemenu/AppTopbarNav') }}></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 w-full min-w-0">
            {# `h2`, not `h1`: two pages already give their own `h1` to the thing
               they are about - a document's title card, a section heading beside
               its New button - and a second `h1` here would leave the outline
               with two tops. #}
            <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold leading-tight text-primary truncate">{{ pageTitle }}</h2>
        </div>

        {# Search and notifications, at the far right. They used to sit in the side
           menu above the nav filter, which put two controls that have nothing to do
           with navigation inside the thing that navigates. #}
        <div class="flex items-center gap-1 pr-3 sm:pr-6 lg:pr-8 shrink-0">
            <div {{ vue_component('core/backend/sidemenu/AppTopbarActions', {
                notificationsListPath: path('backend_notifications_list'),
                notificationsMarkReadPath: path_template('backend_notifications_mark_read', {id: '__id__'}),
                notificationsMarkAllReadPath: path('backend_notifications_mark_all_read'),
                notificationsDeletePath: path_template('backend_notifications_delete', {id: '__id__'}),
                notificationsDeleteAllPath: path('backend_notifications_delete_all'),
            }) }}></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# The trail, at every size. On a phone this is the whole header, and 24px
       shorter than the single band it replaces - which is also why the levels
       above the last two step aside below `sm`: there is no second band to fall
       back on, and a deep path would push the page it names off the screen. #}
    {% if trail %}
        <div class="flex items-center h-10 bg-surface border-b border-line">
            <nav class="px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 w-full min-w-0 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm" aria-label="{{ 'backend.nav.breadcrumb'|trans }}">
                {% for crumb in trail %}
                    {% set isNarrowVisible = loop.index >= (trail|length - 1) %}
                    {% if not loop.first %}
                        <span class="text-muted select-none shrink-0 {% if not isNarrowVisible %}hidden sm:inline{% endif %}">/</span>
                    {% endif %}
                    <span class="min-w-0 {% if not isNarrowVisible %}hidden sm:inline{% endif %}">
                        {% if crumb.href is defined and crumb.href %}
                            <a href="{{ crumb.href }}" class="text-secondary hover:text-primary transition-colors truncate block">{{ crumb.label }}</a>
                        {% elseif loop.last %}
                            <span class="text-secondary font-medium truncate block" aria-current="page">{{ crumb.label }}</span>
                        {% else %}
                            <span class="text-secondary truncate block">{{ crumb.label }}</span>
                        {% endif %}
                    </span>
                {% endfor %}
            </nav>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</header>
